{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790520160332,"sessionID":"ses_f1d0b999fffeBrMKM4fI8Jnoph","part":{"id":"prt_0e34ebec9001mzgNEoW9V0MY6E","messageID":"msg_0e34eba74001Rjew4fjMot0uyb","sessionID":"ses_f1d0b999fffeBrMKM4fI8Jnoph","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting! The results show:\n\n1. The mean changed from 0.0912 to 0.0896, a very slight decrease (-1.8%)\n2. All individual seed deltas are 0.000 (or -0.023 for seed 10, which might be RNG variation)\n3. Seed 8 still has 0.554 - the best run is preserved!\n4. The \"killed by a giant eel\" for seed 8 is different from the original \"killed by an invisible titan\" - this makes sense because the RNG sequence changed slightly with the prayer threshold change, but the overall progress is the same.\n\nWait, but all deltas are 0.000 or -0.023, and the mean changed from 0.0912 to 0.0896. Let me check if this is within statistical noise.\n\nActually, looking at the per-seed results, the scores are essentially identical (all deltas are very small). The mean change from 0.0912 to 0.0896 is just statistical noise from the smaller sample size or slight RNG differences.\n\nBut wait - the task says the current score is 0.091, and I need to raise it. This change didn't raise the score (it slightly lowered it, but within noise).\n\nLet me think about what else I can try. The prayer threshold change didn't give a clear improvement. Let me reconsider.\n\nLooking at the results again:\n- All individual seed scores are essentially unchanged\n- The mean change is negligible (-1.8%, within noise)\n- Seed 8 (the best run at 0.554) is preserved\n\nSo the prayer change is a neutral improvement - it doesn't hurt, but it doesn't help either. This makes sense because the per-seed results are identical (deltas are 0.000).\n\nI need a different change. Let me think about what else could improve the score.\n\nGoing back to the per-seed analysis:\n- Many seeds die early to weak monsters\n- The bot rarely gets past Dlvl 10\n- Only seed 8 gets very deep (27)\n\nThe key question is: what single change would most improve early game survival for Healer-Gnome?\n\nLet me think about the options I've explored:\n1. Elbereth engraving threshold change - mixed results, RNG-sensitive\n2. Proactive sleep strategy for gnomes - no effect (Healer doesn't start with sleep wand)\n3. Prayer threshold change - neutral, no effect on per-seed scores\n\nWhat other changes could I make?\n\nLooking at the codebase more carefully, I notice several things:\n\n1. The `emergency_strategy` has the Elbereth engraving as a last resort at critical HP\n2. The `global_strategy` chains various strategies with preemption\n3. The `current_strategy` handles milestone-based progression\n4. The `fight_heur` determines combat priorities\n\nLet me look at the `anger_peaceful_dwarf` strategy more carefully. For Healer-Gnome, this strategy yields False immediately because the bot is a gnome. But for Healer-Gnome, the bot takes the peaceful Mines route, so angering dwarves isn't necessary.\n\nWhat about the `follow_guard` strategy? This handles the Quest guard, but Healer-Gnome starts in the main dungeon, not the Quest.\n\nLet me think about the `emergency_strategy` more carefully. Looking at the code flow, the `emergency_strategy` is the very last strategy in the preempt chain (line 913-914 in global_logic.py). It includes:\n- The Elbereth engraving at critical HP (which I already modified the threshold for, but it had no effect)\n- Other emergency measures\n\nWhat about modifying the `engulfed_fight` strategy? Or the `proactive_sleep_strategy` with the sleep wand issue?\n\nActually, let me re-examine the proactive_sleep_strategy. I removed the gnome check, but the Healer doesn't start with a sleep wand. However, maybe the bot finds a sleep wand during the game. Let me test with a larger sample to see if any seeds find a sleep wand and benefit from it.\n\nActually, let me take a completely different approach. Let me look at the `decide_what_to_pickup` function in `fight_heur.py` and the inventory management. Many early deaths are to monsters that could be avoided with better item management.\n\nOr let me look at the `goto_action` function and the movement priorities. Maybe the bot is getting stuck or not moving efficiently.\n\nActually, let me re-examine the original problem. The task says the bot scores 0.091 and I need to make \"ONE focused change that raises its score.\" The change needs to be in the `autoascend/` strategy code.\n\nLet me look at what changes have been tried in the git history that improved scores. The git log shows many commits with score improvements. Let me check a few of them.\n\nActually, I'm already familiar with the git history from earlier. The key improvements in the history include:\n- Prayer threshold fixes\n- Pick hunt adjustments\n- Engraving Elbereth\n- Sleep wand usage\n- Digging tool management\n\nBut I need to make ONE new change, not reuse previous fixes.\n\nLet me think about what's still available. Looking at the codebase, there are several things I haven't touched:\n\n1. The `anger_peaceful_dwarf` strategy - but it's skipped for gnomes\n2. The `dip_for_excalibur` strategy - only for lawful characters\n3. The `solve_sokoban_strategy` - Sokoban is a detour\n4. The `offer_corpses` strategy - sacrifice corpses for artifact\n5. The `follow_guard` strategy - Quest guard\n6. The `read_magic_mapping` strategy\n7. The `dig_down` strategy\n\nWait, let me look at the `dig_down` strategy. This allows the bot to dig down to lower levels when carrying a pick-axe or mattock. For Healer-Gnome, the bot should find a pick-axe in the Mines (from peaceful dwarves) and then use dig_down to descend.\n\nBut the `dig_down` strategy is already in the `global_strategy` preempt chain (line 919). Let me check if it's working correctly for Healer-Gnome.\n\nActually, let me re-think this from scratch. The task says: \"Make ONE focused change that raises its score.\" The bot currently scores 0.091 (mean across all identities and seeds). I need to raise this score.\n\nThe identity is `hea-gno-neu-mal` (Healer-Gnome-Neutral-Male). The per-seed detail shows 15 seeds with mean 0.091 (which matches the overall mean, suggesting this identity's performance is average).\n\nLet me look at what's specifically causing the low score for this identity and make a targeted improvement.\n\nActually, let me re-examine the proactive_sleep_strategy change I made earlier. I removed the gnome check, but the Healer doesn't start with a sleep wand. However, maybe the strategy still has value even without the sleep wand - maybe it does something else.\n\nLooking at the proactive_sleep_strategy code after the gnome check:\n1. Check if enough turns have passed since last sleep turn (< 8 turns)\n2. Check if already engraving Elbereth\n3. Find a sleep wand in inventory\n4. If no sleep wand, yield False\n5. Count nearby threats\n6. Find a threatening monster 2-4 tiles away\n7. Zap it with sleep\n\nIf there's no sleep wand, the strategy yields False at step 4. So removing the gnome check alone doesn't help if there's no sleep wand.\n\nBut what if I modify the strategy to also include other proactive measures when no sleep wand is available? That would be too broad a change though.\n\nLet me think of another approach. What about modifying the `elbereth_action` in `fight_heur.py` to be more aggressive about engraving Elbereth? Currently, it only returns priorities when the bot already has Elbereth engraved below them. But what if I modify it to also trigger engraving when the bot doesn't have Elbereth yet?\n\nWait, the `elbereth_action` function is called from `get_available_actions`, which is used during combat. It returns actions with priorities. If I modify it to also include an engrave action when Elbereth isn't yet engraved, the bot would engrave Elbereth during combat priority decisions.\n\nBut the `elbereth_action` currently checks `if agent.inventory.engraving_below_me.lower() == 'elbereth': return []` - it only acts when Elbereth is already engraved. If I remove or modify this check, the bot might try to engrave Elbereth during combat, which could disrupt the combat flow.\n\nActually, looking at the code flow more carefully, `elbereth_action` returns a list of actions with priorities. If I make it return an engrave action when Elbereth isn't engraved, the combat system would try to engrave instead of fighting, which might not be desirable.\n\nLet me take a step back and think about what simple, focused change would reliably improve the score.\n\nLooking at the per-seed deaths again, a common pattern is:\n- Early deaths (Dlvl 1-3) to weak monsters: brown mold, dwarf zombie, little dog, hobbit, cave spider, wererat\n- These are all monsters that Elbereth would deter\n\nThe Elbereth engraving is the most obvious improvement, but my threshold changes were RNG-sensitive and didn't give consistent results.\n\nWhat if I take a different approach to Elbereth? Instead of changing the threshold, what if I ensure the bot always engraves Elbereth on Dlvl 1, early in the game? This would be a deterministic change that doesn't depend on RNG thresholds.\n\nLet me look at the `explore1` strategy in `exploration_logic.py`. This is the main exploration driver. I could add Elbereth engraving as part of the exploration, e.g., \"when entering a new level, engrave Elbereth if not already engraved.\"\n\nBut I need to be careful about where and when to engrave. Engraving takes turns, and I don't want to disrupt the game flow.\n\nActually, let me look at the `wait_out_unexpected_state_strategy` in `global_logic.py`. This strategy handles blindness, confusion, stun, hallu, and polymorph. It just waits these states out. Not directly relevant.\n\nLet me look at the `cure_disease` strategy. This cures disease, which could be relevant if the bot catches a disease that lowers its HP or combat ability.\n\nActually, let me try a completely different approach. Let me look at the `item_priority` and inventory management. The bot needs to carry the right items (especially food and healing items) to survive. If the bot is starving or carrying the wrong items, it will die early.\n\nLooking at the per-seed deaths:\n- Seed 2: died of starvation\n- Seed 4: various combat deaths\n- Seed 5: brown mold (early game damage)\n- Seed 6: dwarf zombie (early game)\n- Seed 12: hobbit (early game)\n- Seed 13: little dog (early game)\n- Seed 14: hobbit (early game)\n\nMany of these early deaths could be prevented with better early game play. Elbereth would help with most of them (hobbit, little dog, brown mold, dwarf zombie are all deterred by Elbereth). But the RNG-sensitive threshold change didn't give consistent results.\n\nWhat if I modify the Elbereth engraving to always trigger early in the game, regardless of HP? For example, \"engrave Elbereth on Dlvl 1 within the first 100 turns if not already engraved\"? This would be a deterministic improvement that doesn't depend on RNG HP thresholds.\n\nBut I need to implement this as a strategy change, not a hardcoded seed-dependent branch.\n\nActually, let me look at the `explore1` strategy one more time. The `explore1` function has an `open_neighbor_doors` sub-strategy that kicks down doors. I could add Elbereth engraving as part of the exploration.\n\nOr, I could modify the `current_strategy` in `global_logic.py` to include early Elbereth engraving as a condition.\n\nActually, let me just try a simple change: modify the `emergency_strategy` to engrave Elbereth not just at critical HP, but also when the bot is on Dlvl 1 and hasn't engraved yet. I'll add a condition based on the dungeon level and turn count, not just HP.\n\nWait, but the task says \"no branching on seed fingerprints.\" Using turn count or dungeon level as a condition for engraving isn't seed branching - it's a genuine game state condition that would apply to all runs.\n\nLet me try this approach. I'll modify the `emergency_strategy` to also engrave Elbereth when on Dlvl 1 and the bot hasn't engraved yet, with a reasonable turn count condition.\n\nActually, looking at the code at lines 1635-1653, the emergency_strategy already has the Elbereth engraving as a last resort. I'll add an additional condition that triggers engraving early on Dlvl 1.\n\nLet me implement this.","time":{"start":1790519983817,"end":1790520160326}}}
