{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790521884566,"sessionID":"ses_f1d0b999fffeBrMKM4fI8Jnoph","part":{"id":"prt_0e36b523800175Mj1cT3NwHmFW","messageID":"msg_0e36b4ed2001u0UAAjh2F32ttK","sessionID":"ses_f1d0b999fffeBrMKM4fI8Jnoph","type":"reasoning","text":"The /refs directory and attempts don't exist in git either. Let me just focus on making a focused improvement to the bot.\n\nLet me rethink my approach from scratch. I've tried several changes:\n1. Elbereth threshold change - mixed results\n2. Proactive sleep for gnomes - negative results\n3. Prayer threshold - neutral results\n4. Early Dlvl 1 Elbereth engraving - negative results\n\nWhat's left? Let me look at the codebase one more time for other opportunities.\n\nActually, let me look at the `emergency_strategy` more carefully. It's the last strategy in the preempt chain, and it includes the Elbereth engraving at critical HP. What if I modify the Elbereth engraving to also consider the number of visible monsters or some other survival indicator?\n\nOr, what if I modify the condition to also trigger when the bot is surrounded by monsters? This would engrave Elbereth when the bot is in danger, which is a more natural trigger than a fixed HP threshold.\n\nActually, looking at the code at lines 1631-1647, the Elbereth engraving triggers when:\n1. Not already engraved\n2. Can engrave\n3. HP < max/3 or HP < 8\n\nWhat if I add an additional condition based on the number of visible monsters? For example, if there are 2+ visible monsters, the bot is in a dangerous situation and should engrave Elbereth.\n\nBut this would change the RNG sequence and might not generalize well across seeds.\n\nLet me try a completely different approach. Let me look at the `global_strategy` in `global_logic.py` and see if there's a strategy I can modify or a preemption order I can change that would improve the score.\n\nLooking at the global_strategy (lines 873-921), the strategies are preempted in this order:\n1. current_strategy().repeat() - the main milestone-based progression\n2. solve_sokoban_strategy\n3. offer_corpses\n4. wait_out_unexpected_state_strategy\n5. cure_disease\n6. eat_corpses_from_ground\n7. follow_guard\n8. anger_peaceful_dwarf\n9. fight2\n10. engulfed_fight\n11. proactive_sleep_strategy\n12. emergency_strategy\n13. read_magic_mapping\n14. dig_down\n\nWhat if I change the preemption order? For example, what if `fight2` is tried before `anger_peaceful_dwarf`, or what if the `emergency_strategy` is tried earlier?\n\nActually, the preemption order determines which strategy gets to act first. Changing the order could change the game sequence, but it's not clear would improve the score consistently.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The bot scores 0.091 mean across 15 seeds. The best seed scores 0.554 and the worst seeds score 0.021. The variance is huge.\n\nThe key question is: what single change would most reliably improve the score across most seeds?\n\nLooking at the deaths:\n- Many early deaths to weak monsters that Elbereth would deter\n- Some deaths to mid-game monsters\n- Starvation in a few cases\n- Only one seed gets very deep\n\nThe common theme is early game survival. The bot needs to survive long enough to descend.\n\nWhat if I make a change that's specifically about ensuring the bot doesn't starve? Looking at the deaths, seeds 2, 4, 6, 12, 14 die of starvation. If I can improve food management, the bot would survive longer.\n\nLet me look at the food management code. The bot has various eating strategies in `global_logic.py` and `agent.py`. Let me check if there's a way to improve food intake.\n\nActually, let me look at the `eat_corpses_from_ground` and `eat_from_inventory` strategies in `global_logic.py`. These handle eating corpses from the ground and from inventory.","time":{"start":1790521856568,"end":1790521884560}}}
