{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790521959067,"sessionID":"ses_f1d0b999fffeBrMKM4fI8Jnoph","part":{"id":"prt_0e36c1b46001cKFkXHSM321P2P","messageID":"msg_0e36c1675001Q6lCY8PukrDrHo","sessionID":"ses_f1d0b999fffeBrMKM4fI8Jnoph","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I understand the hunger states:\n- SATIATED = 0 (well fed)\n- NOT_HUNGRY = 1 (not hungry)\n- HUNGRY = 2 (hungry)\n- WEAK = 3 (weak from hunger)\n- FAINTING = 4 (about to faint)\n\nThe `eat_from_inventory` strategy triggers when `hunger_state < HUNGRY` yields False, meaning it only acts when `hunger_state >= HUNGRY`. And it eats the first food item in inventory.\n\nThe `eat_corpse_from_ground` strategy is in the `global_strategy` preempt chain.\n\nLet me think about what change to make. The starvation deaths suggest the bot isn't eating enough. But modifying food management might be tricky because NetHack's food mechanics are complex.\n\nLet me step back and think about what single, focused change would most reliably improve the score. I've tried several things already. Let me re-examine the problem.\n\nThe bot scores 0.091 mean across 15 seeds of hea-gno-neu-mal. The key issues are:\n1. Early deaths to weak monsters (most seeds)\n2. Starvation (5 seeds)\n3. Not getting deep (only seed 8 does well)\n\nThe most impactful change would be to improve early game survival, which means preventing deaths to weak monsters. Elbereth is the most important tool for this, but my threshold changes had mixed results.\n\nWhat if I try the Elbereth threshold change one more time, but with a very specific, moderate adjustment? The original was `1/5 or < 5`, and I tried `1/3 or < 8` (which was neutral) and `1/2 or < 15` (which helped some seeds but hurt others). What about `0.4 or < 10` - a middle ground?\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. The Elbereth engraving in `emergency_strategy` is a last-resort measure. It only triggers when ALL other strategies have been exhausted. So even if I change the threshold, it still only fires as a last resort.\n\nWhat if the issue is that the Elbereth engraving simply doesn't trigger often enough? Maybe I should look at why it doesn't trigger and address that.\n\nLooking at the conditions:\n1. `self.inventory.engraving_below_me.lower() != 'elbereth'` - must not already be engraved\n2. `self.can_engrave()` - must be able to engrave (not polymorphed, etc.)\n3. `self.blstats.hitpoints < 1 / 3 * self.blstats.max_hitpoints or self.blstats.hitpoints < 8` - must be at critical HP\n\nCondition 3 is the bottleneck. At XL 1 with max HP ~15, this means HP < 5 or HP * 3 < 15, i.e., HP < 5 or HP < 5, so HP <= 4. At XL 5 with max HP ~25, HP < 5 or HP * 6 < 25, i.e., HP < 5 or HP <= 4, so HP <= 4. At XL 8 with max HP ~35, HP < 5 or HP * 7 < 35, i.e., HP < 5 or HP <= 4, so HP <= 4.\n\nWait, that can't be right. Let me recalculate.\n\n`critically_low_hp` returns `hp < max_hp / 3 or hp < 8` with my change. At XL 1 with max_hp = 15: `hp < 5 or hp < 8` = `hp <= 4 or hp <= 7` = `hp <= 4`. So prayer triggers at HP <= 4.\n\nWith the old divisor-based threshold at XL 1: divisor = 5, max_hp = min(max_hitpoints, 15) = 15. Returns `hp <= 5 or hp * 5 <= 15` = `hp <= 5 or hp <= 3` = `hp <= 3`. So prayer only triggered at HP <= 3.\n\nSo my change from `1/5 or < 5` to `1/3 or < 8` actually made the threshold more generous (HP <= 4 vs HP <= 3 at XL 1). But the per-seed results showed no delta, meaning the prayer wasn't being triggered at all in those seeds, or it didn't affect the outcome.\n\nHmm, let me think about why the prayer isn't triggering. The prayer check is at line 1606-1612 in the `update` method:\n```python\nif (\n    (self.is_safe_to_pray(500) and self.critically_low_hp())\n    or (self.is_safe_to_pray(400) and hunger_prayer_due)\n):\n    yield True\n    self.pray()\n    return\n```\n\nFor the prayer to trigger, BOTH `is_safe_to_pray(500)` must be True AND `critically_low_hp()` must be True. The `is_safe_to_pray(500)` requires that either:\n- This is the first prayer and turn > 110, OR\n- A prayer was said more than 500 turns ago\n\nSo on a new game, the first prayer can trigger at turn 111. But most games that last 111 turns will have the prayer trigger. However, many games end before turn 111 (especially the early deaths we see).\n\nActually, looking at the per-seed results, many games end very quickly (turns 2000-30000, but many at turns 1000-5000). If the game ends before turn 111, the first prayer can never trigger.\n\nBut wait, the `hunger_prayer_due` check at line 1608 might trigger earlier: `is_safe_to_pray(400) and hunger_prayer_due`. The `hunger_prayer_due` includes `self.blstats.hitpoints * 2 < self.blstats.max_hitpoints` at line 1602, which means hunger prayer triggers when HP is less than half. This is a much more frequent trigger.\n\nBut the hunger prayer is for when the bot is fainting from hunger, not for general HP management. And the per-seed starvation deaths suggest the bot isn't eating enough, not that it's not praying enough.\n\nOK, I'm going in circles. Let me take a completely different approach and look at what specific improvement the NetHack community has identified as most impactful for Healer-Gnome.\n\nActually, let me just try one more thing. Let me modify the Elbereth engraving to have a slightly higher threshold, and also add a condition based on the number of visible monsters. This might make the engraving trigger more often in dangerous situations.\n\nNo wait, I've been going back and forth too much. Let me just pick one change and test it thoroughly.\n\nMy best idea so far is to modify the `critically_low_hp` function to use a more generous threshold, which would allow the Healer to pray more frequently. Even though the 5-seed and 15-seed tests showed no delta, maybe the effect is visible with a larger sample or a different threshold.\n\nActually, let me re-examine the 15-seed prayer test. The mean went from 0.0912 to 0.0896, which is a very small change (-1.8%). And all individual deltas were 0.000 (or -0.023 for seed 10). This suggests the prayer threshold change had essentially no effect on the per-seed scores.\n\nOK, let me try a completely different approach. What about modifying the `current_strategy` in `global_logic.py` to ensure the bot leaves the Dlvl 1 grind earlier? Looking at the condition:\n\n```python\ncondition = lambda: self.agent.blstats.experience_level >= self._grind_xl() or \\\n    (LEAVE_GRIND_WHEN_HUNGRY and\n     self.agent.character.role in ROLES_LEAVING_GRIND_WHEN_HUNGRY and\n     self.agent.blstats.hunger_state >= Hunger.HUNGRY and\n     self.agent.inventory.items.total_nutrition() == 0)\n```\n\nFor Healer, `role in ROLES_LEAVING_GRIND_WHEN_HUNGRY` is False. So the bot only leaves when `experience_level >= _grind_xl()`. For Healer, `_grind_xl()` defaults to `GRIND_XL = 8`.\n\nWhat if I add Healer to `ROLES_LEAVING_GRIND_WHEN_HUNGRY`? This would allow the bot to leave the Dlvl 1 grind when hungry, which could help it descend sooner and score better.\n\nBut I need to be careful. The `LEAVE_GRIND_WHEN_HUNGRY` flag is used to control whether roles should leave the grind when hungry. Adding Healer to this list might cause the bot to leave the grind too early, before gaining enough XP, and die from lack of experience.\n\nActually, looking at the comment in the code: \"ROLES_LEAVING_GRIND_WHEN_HUNGRY = {Character.ARCHEOLOGIST, Character.BARBARIAN, Character.CAVEMAN, Character.RANGER, Character.SAMURAI, Character.MONK, Character.WIZARD}\". Healer is not in this list, presumably because Healers do better finishing the grind (they have healing prayer and can survive longer on Dlvl 1).\n\nBut if I add Healer, the bot would leave the grind when hungry, which might be beneficial or detrimental depending on the situation.\n\nLet me test this change.","time":{"start":1790521908038,"end":1790521959061}}}
